There's no difference in readability between `map` and a for-loop here actually, the difference is in purpose. You're transforming one array into another and you're not using the intermediate values at all.

`map` is designed exactly for this use-case. If you use a for-loop, you have to create an empty array, push to it, etc. If you use map, you don't. This isn't a level of abstraction that makes it less readable, it's the right level of abstraction.

People frequently misunderstand what `map` does, which makes it seem "harder" than it is. If you're struggling with it, let me know and I'll explain it and show you examples.

Also, let's start with the premise that there's no general "need" to replace `map` with a for-loop just because you think it's simpler to read. This is the same as saying we should replace `reduce` with a for-loop because you can read a for-loop more easily. But `reduce` was made for this purpose too.

So before you go removing `map`, start from "What's the purpose of this code?" and "Is there a tool designed for that purpose?" If yes, use that tool unless it's demonstrably more complex.

